{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>The one lamb you shall prepare in the morning.</b> Since it says ״וְאֵת הַכֶּבֶשׂ הַשֵּׁנִי״, it should have said ״אֶת הַכֶּבֶשׂ הָרִאשׁוֹן״ — and why did it mention the morning burnt offering only once, while that of between the evenings it mentioned twice? And so you will find also in parashat וְאַתָּה תְּצַוֶּה (Exodus 29:39–41)." "And it appears to me to resolve this along the way the Sages of blessed memory said (Tanhuma Pinchas 13): ״צֶדֶק יָלִין בָּהּ״ — no person lodged in Jerusalem with a transgression in his hand; the morning tamid atones for the transgressions of the entire night, and the tamid of between the evenings atones for the transgressions of the entire day. And Scripture says (Micah 2:1): ״הַחוֹשְׁבִים רָע עַל מִשְׁכְּבוֹתָם בְּאוֹר הַבֹּקֶר יַעֲשׂוּהָ״ — from here we learn that the night is not a time of action but only a time for the musings and imaginings of workers of iniquity, and the sin of musing is one and has no second, for the musing can occur without action. Therefore it says: ״אֶת הַכֶּבֶשׂ הָאֶחָד תַּעֲשֶׂה בַבֹּקֶר״, for it atones for those who scheme evil upon their beds. And it is possible that he may relent from his evil and not carry his evil thought into action; therefore the language of ״אֶחָד״ is used for it, for there is one and no second to it. And therefore it mentioned the morning burnt offering only once. But the sin of the day is undoubtedly doubled: even though he thought about it at night to do it, nevertheless during the day at the time of action he again thinks about it, for without doubt he did not perform the deed without thought and intention — for if so, he is neither intentional nor inadvertent but rather coerced. Therefore the tamid of between the evenings needs to atone for two transgressions — for the musing and for the deed; therefore it is mentioned twice in the passage.\n\nAnd as for what they said: no person lodged in Jerusalem with an offense in his hand — why did they not say, no person rises from his bed with an offense in his hand? Because the language of ״וּבְיָדוֹ״ implies that it is speaking of a sin of action performed literally with the hands; and if so, at night he never has an offense in his hand, for the musing is not in his hand. Rather, the force of the statement comes to teach us that the tamid atones even for the day's transgressions, even though they contain both musing and deed — and all the more so the morning tamid, which atones for the musing alone." "And it says: ״שְׁנַיִם לַיּוֹם עוֹלָה תָמִיד״ — it should have said ״עוֹלוֹת תָּמִיד״. And it appears that from the force of this difficulty they said that each one atones for its own distinct matter, for after all, both of them are one burnt offering — so why did Scripture divide them into two times? Because the morning one atones for the night's transgressions and the evening one atones for the day's.\n\nNow, according to our approach, it appears we may explain as follows: because it says ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר יוֹם אֶחָד״, and above in the parashah of Genesis (1:5) we wrote that it says ״יוֹם אֶחָד״ for this reason — to remove from the hearts of the heretics who say that He who created morning did not create evening, for from a single beginning two opposites cannot emerge; therefore it says ״יוֹם אֶחָד״ — that the evening and the morning are all the day of the One, blessed be He, for one El created them both. So too it is said of these offerings: ״עוֹלָה תָמִיד״ — for both of them are one burnt offering to one El." "And the fact that the morning tamid is slaughtered in the west — to indicate that it atones for the night's transgressions; and the tamid of between the evenings is slaughtered in the east — to indicate that it atones for the transgressions of the entire day. And there are those who say that the reversal of the place of their slaughter is to demolish the notion of the sun-worshippers and moon-worshippers — and all is for one El, blessed be He, and blessed be His name, may He be blessed."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {1 "The word וּבְיָדוֹ in the second paragraph is inverted in the source relative to the first paragraph's וַעֲבֵרָה בְּיָדוֹ; both rendered as 'in his hand' following the Midrashic phrasing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:38.005377136Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}